<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pier Luigi Farnese</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pier Luigi Farnese was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>, who created for him the Duchy of Castro in 1537 and the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza in 1545. His harsh rule over the Piacenza nobility made him enemies, and he was assassinated in 1547 in a conspiracy backed by the imperial governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. His descendants ruled Parma until 1731.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pier-luigi-farnese.jpg" alt="Pier Luigi Farnese" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pier_Luigi_Farnese_di_Tiziano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pier Luigi Farnese was born on 19 November 1503, the illegitimate son of Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Alessandro Farnese</a>, later Pope Paul III, and Silvia Ruffini. Pope Julius II legitimised him in 1505 so that he could inherit the Farnese estates, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> did so again in 1513, but his birth troubled him all his life. His father arranged his marriage to Gerolama Orsini, which took place in 1519. He became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> soldier in the pay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1520 and then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> until 1527, and was present at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the sack he quartered his men in the family palace, which saved it. After it he taxed the Roman countryside heavily, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> threatened him with excommunication. In 1528 he fought for the emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. When his father became pope in 1534, Paul III made Pier Luigi's son Alessandro a cardinal and on 31 January 1537 named Pier Luigi Captain General of the Church. The pope then created the Duchy of Castro for him, formed from the family lands around Castro and Ronciglione, and he commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-sangallo-the-younger">Antonio da Sangallo the Younger</a> to plan a new capital there. The duchy remained under the direct control of the Holy See.</p><p>In 1538 his son Ottavio married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-parma">Margaret of Parma</a>, an illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and in 1543 his son Orazio married Diane de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, an illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a>. In 1545 Paul III made Pier Luigi Duke of Parma and Piacenza, which had been part of the Papal States, in exchange for the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> and Nepi and a yearly payment of 9,000 ducats to the Holy See. Pier Luigi took possession on 23 September 1546.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His tyrannical rule and his taxes made him enemies in the cities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> encouraged the discontent, partly in order to annex Parma and Piacenza to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Ferrante Gonzaga, the governor of Milan, organised a plot with Louis Gonzaga and Count Giovanni Anguissola, who was joined by other noblemen. Pier Luigi was stabbed to death at Piacenza on 10 September 1547 and his body hung from a window of his palace. Gonzaga then took the duchy, and Paul III accused him of the murder. The duchy returned to Pier Luigi's son Ottavio in 1551. Pier Luigi's body was buried at Piacenza and later taken to the family tomb on the island of Bisentina on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena">Lake Bolsena</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pier Luigi Farnese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1545 Pope Paul III, born Alessandro Farnese, detached them from the Church and created a duchy for his son <b>Pier Luigi Farnese</b>, who already held the small papal fief of Castro north of Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1537 Pope Paul III, born Alessandro Farnese, carved the Duchy of Castro out of the Papal States for his son <b>Pier Luigi Farnese</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-castro">Duchy of Castro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Piacenza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1531" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pier Luigi Farnese"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1531">What was here in 1531? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
